To make a class destructible, you need to use a closure variable for `this`. Here is a standard way to do it:

```java
public class PtrClass {
    private int value;
    private PtrClass() {
        this.value = 1;
    }

    // This captures the value of the instance so the closure can access it later
    public PtrClass(int value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    public void destroy() {
        // This closes over the value of this instance from the outer scope
        this.value = 0; 
        // the object no longer exists
    }
}
```

Alternatively, if you prefer not to use a closure, you can use a static factory method. This is often faster for generic code:

```java
public static final PtrClass<T> PtrClass(T t) {
