When the kernel receives a SYN packet, it sets up a receive queue and attaches a TCB to the receive socket. If it skips this step, the TCB remains uninitialized. When the kernel later calls `recv` on that queue, it tries to post a message to a TCB that doesn't actually exist in memory. This triggers a segmentation fault (SIGSEGV) in the kernel.

