The Dirty Pipe vulnerability lets a process write to another process's memory. Here is a minimal C example:

```c
#include <linux/unistd.h>
#include <linux/vmlinux.h>
#include <linux/pipe.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>

int main(void) {
    DESTROY_DEAD_WAIT(0);
    uint32_t mode = 0;
    uint32_t count = 0;

    // Mock a file descriptor
    while (1) {
        // Write to fd 2 (pipe)
        fd = open("/tmp/pipe", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_RDWR);
        write(fd, 2, 0, 0);
        mode = mode + 1;
        count = 2;
    }

    // Read the mode back
    int mode = open("/tmp/pipe", O_RDONLY | O_CREAT | O_RDWR);
    read(fd, 1, 0, mode, 0);
    mode = mode + 1;
    count = 1;
}
```

This shows how a process can use `write()` to modify a file descriptor in another process. The critical issue is that `write()` does not automatically update the `st_mode` field, so the target process reads the original value instead of the changed one. The `unix` module exposes this by having no restriction on writing to file descriptors.

Dirty Pipe is a trapdoor that breaks input validation. It lets an attacker control the kernel's input buffer to run arbitrary code. Here is a minimal, functional exploit that demonstrates the mechanism:

```c
